---
/*  The parts every bambi window has, and the gestures each takes. The pictures are the plugins' own,
    drawn by their picture tools; the text names colours by what they mean, not by their hue. */
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const img = (name: string) => `${base}/tutorial/${name}.webp`;

const parts = [
  ['header', 'the header'],
  ['scene', 'the scene'],
  ['matrix', 'the modulation matrix'],
  ['source', "a source's page"],
  ['panel', 'the panel and a value'],
  ['regions', 'regions'],
  ['presets', 'presets'],
  ['link', 'the link'],
  ['settings', 'settings'],
  ['levels', 'levels and the footer'],
];
---

<Page title="tutorial" description="The parts of a bambi window, and how to use them.">
  <h1>tutorial</h1>
  <p class="sub label">the parts of a bambi window, and how to use them</p>

  <div class="prose">
    <p class="lede">Every bambi plugin is laid out the same way, so what you learn in one works in the others.</p>
    <p>
      At the top is the header. Below it on the left is the scene, a picture of the sphere around you, and
      under that the modulation matrix. On the right are the plugin's own tabs, and at the far right its
      levels. This page walks through each part: what it is for, and what you can do with it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <figure>
    <img src={img('window')} alt="An echo window: header, scene, matrix, the taps tab and the level column." width="2040" height="1200" />
    <figcaption class="label">an echo window</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <nav class="contents" aria-label="on this page">
    <ol>
      {parts.map(([id, name]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{name}</a></li>)}
    </ol>
  </nav>

  <section id="header">
    <h2>the header</h2>
    <figure>
      <img src={img('header')} alt="The header of an encoder window with five instance tabs." width="2040" height="88" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>From left to right:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The mark and the plugin's name.</strong> Clicking them takes the window back to its own
          instance when it is showing another one.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Undo and redo</strong>, the two arrows. Every edit can be undone, including a preset load and a
          drag in the scene.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The instances, as tabs.</strong> Every instance of this plugin in your project has a tab, named
          after it. Click one and the whole window turns to that instance: the matrix, the tabs and undo all
          edit it from here. The open tab is the black block. With many instances the tabs shrink, then scroll
          with the mouse wheel.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>‹ preset · name ›</code></strong>. The chevrons step through the presets, and the name
          opens the preset browser. A <code>*</code> after the name means you have changed the sound since
          loading it.
        </li>
        <li><strong>The link</strong>: which session this instance is in, and how many instances share it.</li>
        <li><strong>Settings</strong>, the last glyph.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="scene">
    <h2>the scene</h2>
    <figure>
      <img src={img('scene')} alt="The globe and the equirect side by side, with the energy picture on and the probe placed." width="1236" height="602" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The scene shows the sphere around the listener twice. On the left is the <strong>globe</strong>, a ball
        you look at from outside. On the right is the <strong>equirect</strong>, the whole sphere unrolled flat:
        left to right is all the way round, bottom to top is floor to ceiling. The letters along the top mark
        back, left, front and right.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Drag the globe</strong> to turn it. <code>top</code>, <code>front</code> and <code>side</code> snap it to a view.</li>
        <li><strong><code>full</code></strong> gives the equirect the whole width.</li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>energy</code></strong> shows where the sound is. What came into the plugin is drawn in
          orange, and what the plugin adds in blue. The picture is off when a window opens, because it is the
          one thing in the window that costs processing.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>regions always</code></strong> draws every region's outline. Turn it off and you see only
          the region whose tab is open.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Click a direction</strong> in the echo or the reverb to place the <strong>probe</strong>, a small
          orange dot. The plugin then draws what it would do to a sound from there: the echo's repeats as they
          travel, or the reverb's reflections where they arrive. The probe is worked out from the settings, not
          from the audio, so it answers even while nothing is playing.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="matrix">
    <h2>the modulation matrix</h2>
    <figure>
      <img src={img('matrix')} alt="The matrix: six feature columns, four target rows, three cells with depth." width="1236" height="510" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The matrix is how something moves by itself. Its <strong>columns are sources</strong>, things that change
        over time. Its <strong>rows are targets</strong>, the parameters they move. A cell is how much one source
        moves one target.
      </p>
      <p>The sources come in tabs:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>features</strong> are measurements of the input: <code>level</code> (how loud),
          <code>attack</code> (how sharp the onsets are), <code>tonal</code> (how sustained or pitched), and
          <code>low</code>, <code>mid</code> and <code>high</code> (how much there is in each part of the
          spectrum). Each reads zero in silence, so silence moves nothing.
        </li>
        <li><strong>sidechain</strong> has the same six, measured on the sidechain input instead.</li>
        <li><strong>generators</strong> are three LFOs and three envelopes.</li>
        <li><strong>region</strong>, in the encoder only, is how far the source is inside its region.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Under each source's name, the thin bar shows what it is sending right now, so you can see what a depth
        would do before you set one. The thicker bar under that is the source's amount, which scales every cell
        in its column.
      </p>
      <h3>making a row</h3>
      <p>
        There is always at least one row. To add another, <strong>click any value</strong> on the plugin's tabs.
        Its row appears with a dashed outline, as a <em>provisional row</em>. Clicking another value replaces it,
        and giving it any depth keeps it. Two clicks and a drag is the whole vocabulary:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>Click the value you want to move, say <code>width</code>. A row appears.</li>
        <li>Click a cell in it, say under <code>tonal</code>. It gets a depth of 0.50.</li>
        <li>Drag the cell up or down to set how much. Now the sound widens as it rings.</li>
      </ol>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Drag a cell</strong> up for more and down for less. A negative depth moves the target the other way.</li>
        <li><strong>Hold shift</strong> while dragging for fine steps.</li>
        <li><strong>Click a cell</strong> to switch it between 0.50 and empty. <strong>Double-click</strong> it to empty it.</li>
        <li>A <strong>dot after a row's name</strong> means that row has depth on another tab.</li>
        <li><strong><code>global amount</code></strong>, at the bottom, scales all modulation at once.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="source" class="split">
    <figure class="panel">
      <img src={img('source')} alt="An LFO's page: its wave, its output, its timing, phase, amount and polarity." width="696" height="1116" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>a source's page</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Click a source's name</strong> at the head of its column, and its settings open as an extra tab on
        the right, named for the source. <code>&lt;</code> and <code>&gt;</code> step to the neighbouring sources,
        and <code>×</code> closes the page. Clicking the name again closes it too.
      </p>
      <p>Every source's page shows its live <code>output</code> near the top. After that it depends on the kind of source:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>An LFO</strong> has its wave (<code>sine</code>, <code>triangle</code>, <code>saw</code>,
          <code>s&amp;h</code>), its timing, <code>phase</code>, <code>amount</code> and <code>polarity</code>.
          Timing is <code>free</code>, a <code>rate</code> in hertz, or <code>sync</code>, a <code>division</code>
          of the bar. Both are shown, and the one not in use is greyed. <code>restart</code> ties the LFO to
          playback, so it starts in the same place every time you press play; <code>continue</code> lets it run
          on.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>An envelope</strong> has two tabs. <code>shape</code> holds attack, decay, sustain and release:
          drag the corners of the graph, or the middle of a line to curve it. <code>trigger</code> says what sets
          it off: MIDI notes, or a feature crossing a threshold.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A feature</strong> has its amount, and <code>level</code> also has a <code>release</code>: how
          quickly it falls when the sound stops.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <code>polarity</code> sets where a source rests. At 0 it only pushes one way, from zero up. At 1 it swings
        both ways, about zero.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="panel" class="split">
    <figure class="panel">
      <img src={img('values')} alt="The encoder's source tab: boxes for trim, displace, speed, direction, mode, width and width range." width="696" height="1116" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>the panel and a value</h2>
      <p>
        The right-hand side holds the plugin's own controls, in tabs organised by what each thing acts on. The
        open tab is the black block. A long page scrolls under its tabs.
      </p>
      <p><strong>Every value is one box</strong>: its name, its number and a bar. The whole box is the control.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Drag</strong> anywhere in it, up for more and down for less. <strong>Hold shift</strong> for fine steps.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Double-click</strong> to return it to its default. On an angle that means exactly zero, and it
          also clears any turning a rate has added.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Click</strong> to give it a row in the matrix. A value that is a choice, like
          <code>direction</code> or <code>mode</code>, steps to its next option instead.
        </li>
        <li>Values that go round, like angles, wrap: drag past one end and they come back in at the other.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Three signs on a box:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>An <strong>underline</strong> under its name, in the plugin's own colour, means something in the matrix moves it.</li>
        <li>A band in the plugin's own colour along the bar shows how far the modulation reaches.</li>
        <li>
          A small <strong>orange mark</strong> shows where the value actually is right now, when that differs from
          what you set: an angle turned by its rate, a source carried along its path. The number and the fill
          always stay what you set, which is what is saved and what automation records.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>A greyed box is not in use right now. One click on the choice above it brings it back.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="regions">
    <h2>regions</h2>
    <figure>
      <img src={img('regions')} alt="Echo's send tab: a spot region drawn in the scene with its handles, the kind row and its settings." width="2040" height="1200" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        A region says <strong>where on the sphere</strong> something happens: which part of the field the echo
        hears, or where the reverb's room comes back. Each plugin has a fixed set, each on a tab of its own: the
        echo has a <code>send</code>, the reverb a <code>send</code> and a <code>return</code>. In the encoder the
        region is a source in the matrix instead (below).
      </p>
      <p>
        A region's tab opens with its kind: <code>everywhere</code>, <code>spot</code>, <code>band</code>,
        <code>sectors</code>, <code>dots</code>, <code>clouds</code> or <code>custom</code>. Under it are that
        kind's own settings, such as a spot's size or a band's elevation, and a <code>softness</code> for how
        gently the edge fades. The kind is chosen once; its settings and the region's orientation can all be
        automated and modulated.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong><code>shape</code> and <code>transform</code></strong> split the tab. <code>shape</code> is the
          kind and its settings. <code>transform</code> is <code>yaw</code>, <code>pitch</code> and
          <code>roll</code>, each beside a rate that keeps it turning, then whether the region lets through what
          is <code>inside</code> or what is <code>outside</code>, and how much.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>In the scene</strong>, a region is drawn in ink: its edge where it is half open, and a light wash
          where it fades. While its tab is open it has handles. Drag the white circle to aim it and the others to
          roll it or move its edge; double-click a handle to reset it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>convert to custom</code></strong> freezes the shape into a grid of weights you can edit
          one by one. The kind and its settings are kept underneath, so you can go back to them.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>copy</code> and <code>paste</code></strong> carry a region to another instance, or to
          another plugin. Paste a reverb's return into an echo's send, and the echo is fed exactly where the room
          comes back. The line under the buttons says what the clipboard holds. A copy is a copy: nothing stays
          connected afterwards.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <figure>
      <img src={img('region-source')} alt="The encoder with its region page open from the matrix's region tab." width="2040" height="1200" />
      <figcaption class="label">in the encoder the region is a source: here, a spot the path passes through</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        In the encoder, the region's value is how far the source is inside it: 1 inside, 0 outside, and in between
        across the soft edge. Route it like any source to make a sound slow down, widen or fall quiet as it
        passes through part of the room.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="presets" class="split">
    <figure class="panel">
      <img src={img('presets')} alt="The preset browser open over the panel: search, factory and user presets, folders, and the four actions." width="800" height="1200" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>presets</h2>
      <p>
        A preset is the <strong>whole sound</strong> of an instance: every parameter, the matrix, the regions, the
        plugin's own settings. It does not include the instance's name or its place in the session.
      </p>
      <p>
        Click the preset name in the header and the browser opens over the panel, so the scene and the matrix
        stay in sight while you try things.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Type to <strong>search</strong>, straight away. The search matches folders as well as names.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>One click loads</strong>, and the browser stays open for the next. <kbd>↑</kbd> and <kbd>↓</kbd>
          walk the list, <kbd>return</kbd> loads, <kbd>escape</kbd> clears the search and then closes.
        </li>
        <li>A load is one step of undo, so a preset you did not like is one undo away.</li>
        <li>
          <code>save</code>, <code>save as</code>, <code>rename</code> and <code>delete</code> are at the bottom. A
          slash in a name makes a folder: saving as <code>drums/tight</code> files it under <code>drums</code>.
        </li>
        <li>Delete asks first, and moves the file to the trash.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <code>factory</code> presets are built into the plugin and cannot be changed. <code>user</code> presets are
        files of your own, shared by every project.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="link">
    <h2>the link</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Every bambi instance in a project joins one <strong>session</strong> by itself, with no setup. The session
        is saved with the project and comes back when you reopen it. The header shows the session and how many
        instances are in it.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Every window can edit every instance</strong> of its plugin. Pick one from the header's tabs and
          the whole window turns to it; undo acts on the instance shown.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Other instances appear in the scene</strong> in grey: in the encoder, every source and its path;
          in every plugin, other instances' regions as dotted outlines. They are only drawn; nothing about them
          reaches the sound.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The link carries edits, never sound.</strong> No instance's audio depends on another instance,
          so freezing a track or bouncing stems gives the same result as the full mix.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="settings">
    <h2>settings</h2>
    <figure>
      <img src={img('settings')} alt="The reverb's settings page: its name, the session, the order, and the reverb's own quality settings." width="2040" height="1200" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The glyph at the end of the header opens settings over the window. On the left is what every instance
        has: its <strong>name</strong>, which you can type (<kbd>return</kbd> or <kbd>escape</kbd> hands the
        keyboard back to your host), the <strong>link</strong> session, and the ambisonic <strong>order</strong>,
        which comes from how many channels the track has. On the right is what only this plugin has, such as the
        reverb's quality while playing and while rendering, and what the host reports.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="levels" class="split levels">
    <figure class="column">
      <img src={img('levels')} alt="The level column: dry and wet levels above the output meter." width="104" height="1116" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>levels and the footer</h2>
      <p>
        The column at the far right holds the plugin's <strong>levels</strong>, in decibels: <code>dry</code> and
        <code>wet</code> in the echo and the reverb, <code>gain</code> in the encoder. They are values like any
        other: drag them, double-click for the default, click to give them a row in the matrix. Under them is the
        <strong>output meter</strong>, marked <code>out</code>, which always shows this window's own instance.
      </p>
      <p>
        Ducking needs no control of its own. Click <code>wet</code>, then set a negative depth under
        <code>level</code>, or under the sidechain's <code>level</code>, and the effect gets out of the way while
        the sound plays. <code>level</code>'s <code>release</code> sets how quickly it comes back.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <strong>footer</strong>, under the panel, holds <code>rates continue</code> and a readout: the
        instance's name, its order, and the share of one processor core it is using. With
        <code>rates continue</code> off, as it starts, everything that turns at a rate (a path, a rotating
        region) holds while the transport is stopped and starts from the same place every time you press play,
        so a bounce sounds like what you heard. Switched on, they run all the time, and a bounce no longer
        repeats exactly what you heard.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <figure>
    <img src={img('footer')} alt="The bottom edge of the window: global amount, has depth on another tab, rates continue, and the readout." width="2040" height="72" />
  </figure>
</Page>

<style>
  .sub {
    margin: 12px 0 var(--section);
  }
  section {
    margin-top: var(--section);
    scroll-margin-top: 16px;
  }
  figure {
    margin: 0 0 24px;
  }
  figure img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border: var(--stroke-rule) solid var(--rule);
  }
  figcaption {
    margin-top: 8px;
    text-transform: none;
  }
  .contents ol {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 6px 20px;
  }
  .contents a {
    font-size: var(--size-label);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-label);
  }
  kbd {
    font-family: inherit;
    font-size: 0.9em;
    padding: 0 0.3em;
    border: var(--stroke-rule) solid var(--rule);
  }

  /*  a panel picture beside its text, one above the other on a phone */
  .split {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 348px) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 40px;
    align-items: start;
  }
  .split.levels {
    grid-template-columns: 52px minmax(0, 1fr);
  }
  .split > figure {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .split .prose h2 {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .split,
    .split.levels {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 16px;
    }
    .split > figure {
      order: 1; /* the heading and the text first, the picture after them */
    }
    .split > .panel {
      max-width: 348px;
    }
    .split > .column {
      width: 52px;
    }
  }
</style>
